# Concentric Radii & Inset Clearance

When cards, input fields, and tags form nested hierarchies, radii and paddings must obey strict geometric laws.

## Concentric Radii Geometric Law

When inner and outer rounded containers are concentric, their corner arcs must remain parallel and equidistant:
$$R_{\text{outer}} = R_{\text{inner}} + \text{Padding}$$
- The outer radius must equal the inner radius plus the gap between them.
- If the inner element hugs the outer border directly (Padding = 0), both radii must be identical.

## The Visual Failure of Non-Concentric Nesting

When nested containers naively reuse the same radius (e.g. outer 12px, inner 12px, gap 8px):
- Straight edges maintain an 8px distance.
- Corner distance balloons to $8 \times \sqrt{2} \approx 11.31\text{px}$!
Some 41% more distance at the corner produces an ungrounded, pinched visual defect where the inner element feels floating or squeezed.

