## Caret Clipping Prevention via Horizontal Inset

In rounded input controls (`TxInput`), the corner curve encroaches into the interior content box.
If the input text or blinking cursor starts too close to the left boundary, the cursor directly slices into the corner curve.

TuffEx mandates the clearance constraint:
$$Padding_{\text{horizontal}} \ge R$$
The horizontal padding must fully cover the corner radius value, placing the initial caret safely beyond the curved boundary.

## 0.65x Optical Vertical Padding

Because type carries font leading (internal line-height buffers), identical horizontal and vertical paddings (e.g. `padding: 8px 8px`) look optically bottom-heavy and unbalanced.

TuffEx applies the optical ratio:
$$Padding_{\text{vertical}} \approx 0.6 \sim 0.75 \times Padding_{\text{horizontal}}$$
For instance, the 26px TxScrubField sets 12px text on a 14.5px line, leaving 5.75px above and below, and keeps the glyph at either end 9px from the edge: a ratio of about 0.64. TxModeChip is a fixed 28px around an 18px line, which leaves 5px above and below against 8px sideways (`height: 28px; padding: 0 8px`), a ratio of 0.625; TxTag's md size writes the same inset as `padding: 5px 8px`.

